@*
    ImageSlot — where a picture will go, and a note of what it should be.

    The landing page ships before its photography does, so every picture on it
    starts as one of these. It is drawn as an unpopulated array: the panel grid
    the Dashboard's nav icon draws, with its cells left as dashed outlines. That
    is the same thing UnderConstruction says about a page with no behaviour yet,
    said here about a frame with no photograph yet. On the landing page a dashed
    line always means "not filled in", and this is the largest instance of it.

    The nameplate is the useful part. A solar panel carries a rating plate on
    its back saying what it is; this one says what the picture should be and the
    size to supply it at, so the brief travels with the hole it has to fill
    rather than living in a document nobody opens.

    Filling a slot is one attribute: pass Src (and Alt) and the placeholder is
    replaced by the image — same box, same cut corner. Width and Height are
    required either way. They reserve the space, so neither state shifts the
    layout when the other arrives.
*@

@if (Src is { Length: > 0 })
{
    @* The ratio is stated here as well as in width/height. Those two attributes
       only reserve space until the file arrives; after that height:auto follows
       the file's own shape, and a photograph supplied at any other ratio would
       resize the slot and shove the page about. With the ratio pinned, a
       mismatched file is cropped to the slot (object-fit) instead. *@
    <img class="slot slot--filled @(Compact ? "slot--compact" : null) @Class"
         style="aspect-ratio: @Width / @Height"
         src="@Src"
         alt="@(Decorative ? string.Empty : Alt)"
         width="@Width"
         height="@Height"
         loading="lazy"
         decoding="async" />
}
else
{
    @* role=img makes the whole slot one object with one name, so a screen
       reader hears "placeholder" once instead of walking the plate's lines. *@
    <div class="slot @(Compact ? "slot--compact" : null) @Class"
         style="aspect-ratio: @Width / @Height"
         role="@(Decorative ? null : "img")"
         aria-hidden="@(Decorative ? "true" : null)"
         aria-label="@(Decorative ? null : $"Placeholder for a {Kind.ToLowerInvariant()}: {Brief}")">

        <span class="slot__array"></span>

        @if (Compact)
        {
            @if (Caption is { Length: > 0 })
            {
                <span class="slot__caption">@Caption</span>
            }
        }
        else
        {
            <span class="slot__plate">
                <b>@Kind</b>
                <span>@Brief</span>
                <span class="numeric">@Width &times; @Height</span>
            </span>
        }
    </div>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The real image. Leave unset and the slot draws its placeholder.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Src { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Alt text for the real image. Unused while the slot is empty.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string Alt { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>
    /// What the picture should show, written for whoever has to go and take it.
    /// Printed on the nameplate, and read out as the placeholder's name.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string Brief { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>Photograph, Portrait, Logo, Screenshot — the plate's first line.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string Kind { get; set; } = "Photograph";

    /// <summary>Intrinsic size to supply the asset at. Also fixes the slot's aspect ratio.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public int Width { get; set; }

    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public int Height { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// For slots too small to carry a nameplate — a portrait, a partner's logo.
    /// The brief is still announced; only the printed plate is dropped.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Compact { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// For a picture that sits beside words already saying what it shows — a
    /// portrait next to the person's name. It leaves the accessibility tree in
    /// both states, the same way FundMark does with an empty Label, so the name
    /// is not preceded by a description of a face.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Decorative { get; set; }

    /// <summary>One or two words centred in a compact slot. Optional.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Caption { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? Class { get; set; }
}
